00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.910 align:start position:0%
four and a half million people are  internally displaced in syria most of  them live in refugee camps here in the  northwest  they depend on food medicine and other  basics that are brought in via the  bubblehouse border crossing with turkey  these food boxes contain rice sugar and  oil none of these are within our means  we rely on these containers  however damascus says delivering  international aid to opposition-held  areas violates its sovereignty it wants  humanitarian relief to be routed through  government-held territory  stopping aid will be a disaster in every  sector including medicine food and  education we urge the international  community to keep politics and  humanitarian aid as separate issues  many believe russia's threat is in  retaliation for western countries  providing support to ukraine during its  month-long invasion  doctors fear what's coming  russian pressure to cut aid would create  a crisis that would force more syrians  to be displaced and die while trying to  survive  more than 4 600 u.n trucks mostly  carrying food have used the crossing in  the first six months of 2022.  the un's babel hawa corridor is the best  it's transparent safe and cost-effective  none of the alternatives are better if  it's closed even preventable deaths may  not be stopped  the un estimates 13.4 million syrians  needed assistance last year up from 11.1  million in 2020.  most goods in northwestern syria are  imported from government controlled  areas or here in turkey but as the lira  loses its value and the food prices soar  because of the war in ukraine it is only  compounding the crisis for millions of  people who are dependent on  international aid cnam kyo solo al  jazeera istanbul
 

